#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6e0ed74f-d678-5d0f-a4bf-3a284621f6d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-738" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "4073dad3-002e-5a92-85a8-d47682964069" :question/math-academy-id "e-739" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/8b630e44d49440844d668f395dd5d85676e1824f283edb86194a88a62c78b3f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$\n\n- $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)  <0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- $f'(-1) >0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- $f''(-1) <0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51552b23-6882-503f-8d22-ca43f255d745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3(x+2)-5(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299991} {:answer/id #uuid "3b88dd7e-147f-5da1-8f0a-3a495f3e5e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3(x+2)-5(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299992} {:answer/id #uuid "dc521d82-5e02-5e2b-b19b-9cd83c08a412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+3(x+2)-5(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993} {:answer/id #uuid "de059b60-27d5-585b-bfcf-514c3ceeabf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299994} {:answer/id #uuid "157284ec-d3cb-5c9f-94f1-e0be0aa4c5fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de059b60-27d5-585b-bfcf-514c3ceeabf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299994} :answer-field/id #uuid "25471a63-f587-533b-8dbb-6eb8f9036d3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a6c97dc-b222-56a6-abb0-4345542b7676" :question/math-academy-id "q-125822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/53b9bfe6c2b3109a14fa42651fe402fc546f6dceb1526459500fbdf537565976.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8178110c-9240-5a77-bed6-92ad53268918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + (x + 3) - (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181281} {:answer/id #uuid "06c0de07-0d85-54c3-9898-e94dee930e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + x + x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181282} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec8771-be98-5a58-902d-830534f0fdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + x - x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181283} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0b35d-2aff-508a-8c55-6b2b73a04ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + (x + 3) - (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181284} {:answer/id #uuid "76b61530-59da-58e7-af01-7c973356bd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + (x + 3) + (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0b35d-2aff-508a-8c55-6b2b73a04ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + (x + 3) - (x + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181284} :answer-field/id #uuid "434a4c4f-1cc0-552f-bb4e-8dafda6e357e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50192933-049d-5805-8ae1-413b4aad1117" :question/math-academy-id "q-15523" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/ef5618f26d499eeffd693d1f0dc674761bcfd5762b9620044b295870983799a0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f.$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-3)}{2}(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-3)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1b5f0d1-9d1c-533b-88eb-c719773d1ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b5f0d1-9d1c-533b-88eb-c719773d1ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299998} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ce07e2-30a2-5714-92e3-eb950bf12f95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "164a6578-b0d2-53ef-82eb-e2200c88e999" :question/math-academy-id "q-347994" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=5+3(x-2)-7(x-2)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{′}(2)?$\n\n${f}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the first-degree term is ${f}^{′}(2).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the first-degree term is $3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae833cdf-b5b0-511b-8481-561f00e040aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae833cdf-b5b0-511b-8481-561f00e040aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300001} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7520d9c-3158-5c78-a3f1-909e5d62ecc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecf46b90-08d9-5b68-ad0b-4fd74bb10bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-349354" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=9$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-1+4(x-9)-13(x-9)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(9)?$\n\n${f}^{″}(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=9,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(9).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-13.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(9)=-13\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(9)=-26.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18df5b77-bfb0-5f2b-9b10-c07700a19c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300004} {:answer/id #uuid "77e1eb4a-6a87-5512-9435-ee5a3b8565f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77e1eb4a-6a87-5512-9435-ee5a3b8565f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300005} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5e284d-aeab-54c1-bf71-3abfe5d3e36c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5f36c02-b274-58eb-84ff-145602e4415e" :question/math-academy-id "q-349356" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-10$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=7-8(x+10)+15(x+10)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-10)?$\n\n${f}^{″}(-10)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=-10,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-10).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $15.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-10)=15\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-10)=30.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de30844-bcc3-5e03-a615-735e6719dd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300008} {:answer/id #uuid "630a6f5d-947c-557b-9d78-8f5c7cdebb98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} {:answer/id #uuid "8afa3abe-23bd-57f0-b5e3-d724b7ddec8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300010} {:answer/id #uuid "89cad238-6ec5-5390-85d5-e0ee31741d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300011} {:answer/id #uuid "81b6df15-6e0b-581f-8341-079669dd56aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8afa3abe-23bd-57f0-b5e3-d724b7ddec8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300010} :answer-field/id #uuid "522628d7-6d15-556b-9782-2cd9802186e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "012b69cf-240d-55ca-a054-35b0a0eb2cf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-50948" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=1+6(x-3)+18(x-3)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=3,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $18.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3)=18\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(3)=36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5dac73-ec22-5f59-b6b4-5af467826122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294401} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef6dd2-41b9-5ce9-a150-e2e384cdbc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294402} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b707c4-8e0c-5a96-87b0-7024112b8190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294403} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4785b1-9940-5c81-bfb9-d993312c70d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294404} {:answer/id #uuid "db86223e-0460-570a-80e8-13ae73d77628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db86223e-0460-570a-80e8-13ae73d77628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294405} :answer-field/id #uuid "c95bab29-2e85-5424-9339-70f5b63e00c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ea253-fa70-53c3-a318-c123823ef429" :question/math-academy-id "q-81063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(1)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(1)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2745e6e-c2a5-54b9-9766-476b0d0b0c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300015} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3330c6-feee-5d98-a31b-83eb7c3bf72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300016} {:answer/id #uuid "433a6a07-9750-58a5-9cea-f7c711503b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300017} {:answer/id #uuid "4b91b616-8349-5b7d-85a4-5666bdb89c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300018} {:answer/id #uuid "d55db6dc-ccdc-58f4-8b4d-3d195a74927b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2745e6e-c2a5-54b9-9766-476b0d0b0c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300015} :answer-field/id #uuid "d288ac14-eb35-57ba-bcd3-9bb50b362c2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fe5434f-fc56-57b4-a573-adba643e201f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83725" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=4+3(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{′}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=-1,$ so the coefficient of the first-degree term is ${f}^{′}(-1).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the first-degree term is $3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee507a4d-15ec-5591-a4c2-935720594528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+2(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300022} {:answer/id #uuid "f17dc244-66f4-5501-b26e-65e8dd5b9616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-3(x-3)-2(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300023} {:answer/id #uuid "a454ed0c-9314-5bb3-b21d-bb2645b7412a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300024} {:answer/id #uuid "4be7cbeb-5cdb-58db-973f-f5931e0997d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+3(x-3)+3(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300025} {:answer/id #uuid "db08922e-91ad-5fa9-a011-81652c6075d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2(x-3)+3(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a454ed0c-9314-5bb3-b21d-bb2645b7412a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300024} :answer-field/id #uuid "a65b570c-e057-57bb-b70e-f413b516d44a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a26b2381-0bbb-5cae-8272-9296c98fffe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83728" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/bb52a7975c917c7fe310eb29ae1daaa3438a906b84771ae5ce6998c055c4e6f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(3)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcfc9eff-a9f5-53da-97fd-bbb458330119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300029} {:answer/id #uuid "762edd02-fbb7-5f82-9f8d-9c4d0784e560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x+2)-2(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300030} {:answer/id #uuid "c74d2c8d-6072-5314-ad5e-2c422f561d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300031} {:answer/id #uuid "c956c25c-c09d-5540-a6df-0bb1ad7324fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+3(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300032} {:answer/id #uuid "3d99aa3b-a82c-5dd7-91df-1f8843ceafdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+(x+2)+2(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcfc9eff-a9f5-53da-97fd-bbb458330119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300029} :answer-field/id #uuid "252a0d36-fd03-5b8f-8aa3-ad6ac91ea3b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd80e409-455c-5dd2-b392-fb0349ca3a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-83729" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/9fe49372324dac217bb8e96ae6f485fe74308184ec9dd197fc98b973560dd8af.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}.\n$$"}]}
